---
// COOKBOOK — the visualization workbench (docs-refactor Phase 5, #1000; plan §6.3/§8 + §3 currency).
//
// The two-tier surface model made operable: SUBSTRATE (setRender, pick-one) and OVERLAY
// (setOverlay, additive), plus the live signal readout and the overlayCanvas requirement the plan
// flags as a silent no-op (§7). The demo is live because the surfaces only exist in a browser.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SeeAlso from '../../../components/SeeAlso.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../../components/StatusChip.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import PlatformSupport from '../../../components/PlatformSupport.astro';
import CookbookDemo from '../../../components/CookbookDemo.astro';
import { RENDER_MODES, OVERLAY_MODES } from '../../../lib/docs-api.ts';

const surfacesJs = `// SUBSTRATE — the base layer, behind your content. One at a time.
field.setRender('streamlines');

// OVERLAY — diagnostic readings, drawn in FRONT. Additive: the engine takes an array,
// so these compose rather than replace each other.
field.setOverlay(['force-vectors', 'grid']);
field.setOverlay('off');            // clear the stack`;

const overlayCanvasJs = `import { createOverlaySurface } from '@fundamental-engine/dom';

// An overlay reading needs a SECOND canvas in front of the page. Without one,
// setOverlay() is a silent no-op — the most common "why is nothing happening".
// <field-root> and the React binding create it for you; a raw createField does not.
const surface = createOverlaySurface(document);   // fixed, click-through, aria-hidden

const field = createField(canvas, {
  host,
  overlayCanvas: surface.canvas,   // core sizes it, honouring dprCap + quality tiers
});`;

const readoutCss = `/* The signal readout: the conversion moment. These are the values the
   engine writes onto a feedback body every frame — the field, in numbers. */
.readout::after {
  content: '--d ' var(--d, 0) ' · coherence ' var(--coherence, 0)
           ' · temperature ' var(--temperature, 0);
}`;

const substrateModes = RENDER_MODES.filter((m) => m.name !== 'none');
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Fundamental — The visualization workbench"
  description="The engine's two-tier surface model made operable: a pick-one substrate (setRender) behind your content, an additive overlay stack (setOverlay) in front of it, the overlayCanvas requirement that makes overlays work, and the live signal readout."
>
  <header class="docs-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Cookbook</p>
    <h1>The visualization <span class="accent">workbench</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The same field can be drawn a dozen ways, and read several more. The surfaces are two tiers
      with genuinely different rules: one <strong>substrate</strong> behind your content, and any
      number of <strong>overlay readings</strong> in front of it. Knowing which is which is most of
      the API.
    </p>
    <StatusChip label="Shipped · demo runs live on this page" tone="stable" />
  </header>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="two-tiers">Two tiers, two rules</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>substrate</strong> is the base layer: the same matter, drawn one way at a time.
      Picking <code>trails</code> replaces <code>dots</code> — there is only ever one base.
    </p>
    <p>
      An <strong>overlay</strong> is a reading drawn on top, and readings <em>compose</em>:
      <code>setOverlay</code> takes an array and the engine draws them in order. So the substrate is
      a radio set and the overlays are checkboxes — a distinction the API makes structurally, not by
      convention.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'TypeScript', lang: 'ts', code: surfacesJs }]} />
    <p>
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-handle-methods" symbol="setRender" />
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-handle-methods" symbol="setOverlay" />
    </p>
    <p>
      The full vocabularies — {substrateModes.length + 1} substrate modes and
      {OVERLAY_MODES.length} overlay readings, each with the platforms that have it — are on the
      <a href="/docs/api/declarative#render">declarative reference</a>. Two names look like
      exceptions and are worth stating here: <code>field-lines</code> is an
      <em>overlay</em> reading, and the heatmap is its own toggle
      (<code>setHeatmap</code>), not a render mode — which is why the
      <code>RenderMode</code> type and what <code>setRender</code> accepts are not the same list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="overlay-canvas">The overlay canvas — the silent no-op</h2>
    <p>
      An overlay draws in <em>front</em> of your content, so it needs a second canvas the engine can
      draw onto. If there isn't one, <code>setOverlay</code> does nothing and says nothing — the
      single most common "the API is broken" report, and it is a wiring gap rather than a bug.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;field-root&gt;</code> and the React component create that surface for you. A raw
      <code>createField</code> does not; <code>createOverlaySurface</code> is the one-call version,
      and it deliberately leaves the backing-store size to core so the DPR cap and quality tiers are
      honoured.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'TypeScript', lang: 'ts', code: overlayCanvasJs }]} />
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="live">The workbench, live</h2>
    <p>
      A contained field with the two tiers wired as they actually behave. Switch the substrate — one
      wins. Toggle the readings — they stack.
    </p>

    <CookbookDemo
      kind="workbench"
      title="Substrate (pick one) · overlay (stack any)"
      note="This demo has no overlay canvas of its own, so the overlay toggles record the stack the field is holding while the substrate is what you can see change — the no-op above, demonstrated rather than described."
    >
      <div class="cb-chip" data-body="attract swirl" data-strength="1.1" data-range="200" data-spin="1.2" data-feedback data-hot>
        a body to draw around
      </div>
      <Fragment slot="controls">
        <button type="button" data-cb-render="dots" aria-pressed="true">dots</button>
        <button type="button" data-cb-render="trails" aria-pressed="false">trails</button>
        <button type="button" data-cb-render="links" aria-pressed="false">links</button>
        <button type="button" data-cb-render="streamlines" aria-pressed="false">streamlines</button>
        <button type="button" data-cb-overlay="grid" aria-pressed="false">+ grid</button>
        <button type="button" data-cb-overlay="force-vectors" aria-pressed="false">+ force-vectors</button>
      </Fragment>
    </CookbookDemo>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="readout">The signal readout</h2>
    <p>
      The workbench's most useful panel is not a picture at all. Every frame the engine writes its
      measured channels onto each feedback body — density, load, lit, and the measured
      thermodynamics. Printing them next to the drawing is the moment the field stops looking like
      an effect and starts reading as an instrument.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'CSS', lang: 'css', code: readoutCss }]} />
    <p>
      Every channel, its range and its cadence are on the
      <a href="/docs/api/declarative#feedback">declarative reference</a>; the
      <a href="/docs/diagnostics">diagnostics page</a> covers the explanatory overlays in depth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SeeAlso
    links={[
      { href: '/docs/cookbook', label: 'All cookbook patterns' },
      { href: '/docs/api/declarative', label: 'Render modes & overlay readings (with parity)' },
      { href: '/docs/diagnostics', label: 'Diagnostic overlays' },
      { href: '/docs/inspector', label: 'Inspector & agent JSON' },
      { href: '/patterns', label: 'The pattern gallery (workbench per pattern)' },
    ]}
  />

  <script>
    import { initCookbookDemos } from '../../../lib/cookbook/demos.ts';
    initCookbookDemos();
  </script>
</DocsLayout>
